1
00:00:00,530 --> 00:00:03,397
In a crooked old town, where the long shadows creep, where

2
00:00:03,417 --> 00:00:06,376
the moon's always full, and the bats never sleep,

3
00:00:06,396 --> 00:00:09,209
there floated a ghost, rather small, rather white, named

4
00:00:09,229 --> 00:00:12,392
Alice... who dreamed about snacks every night.

5
00:00:12,412 --> 00:00:14,339
ALICE: Mmm... an eyeball gobstopper...

6
00:00:14,359 --> 00:00:16,210
ALICE: and a stripey moon-mint...

7
00:00:16,230 --> 00:00:17,385
ALICE: Yum.

8
00:00:17,996 --> 00:00:21,051
Now the trouble with ghosts, as the old stories tell, is

9
00:00:21,071 --> 00:00:23,883
they're made out of moonlight, and whispers as well.

10
00:00:23,903 --> 00:00:27,483
So whatever she nibbled, and whatever she chewed...

11
00:00:30,221 --> 00:00:31,413
went straight through the middle.

12
00:00:31,433 --> 00:00:33,550
Poor Alice. No food.

13
00:00:33,570 --> 00:00:36,766
ALICE: Okay. Okay. This time... I'll be really quick.

14
00:00:40,435 --> 00:00:42,415
ALICE: ...Every single time.

15
00:00:44,699 --> 00:00:47,367
SIMMER: Hmm... That's the third gobstopper this week that's rolled

16
00:00:47,387 --> 00:00:48,988
SIMMER: under my belly, dearie.

17
00:00:49,008 --> 00:00:51,776
ALICE: Oh! Granny Simmer! I'm sorry, I didn't mean to wake you...

18
00:00:51,796 --> 00:00:53,655
ALICE: I was just... hungry.

19
00:00:53,675 --> 00:00:57,011
SIMMER: Hungry? Ghosts don't get hungry, little one.

20
00:00:57,031 --> 00:00:58,933
ALICE: Then what's this? Right here.

21
00:00:58,953 --> 00:01:02,346
ALICE: It feels like a hole... like I'm empty in the middle, and

22
00:01:02,366 --> 00:01:04,708
ALICE: everything just falls right through.

23
00:01:04,865 --> 00:01:08,245
SIMMER: Ah. ...That's a different kind of hungry.

24
00:01:08,365 --> 00:01:11,045
ALICE: Every Moon Supper, the whole town sits together at the

25
00:01:11,065 --> 00:01:13,720
ALICE: long table. They pass the bowls...

26
00:01:13,740 --> 00:01:16,195
ALICE: they say 'yum' with their mouths full...

27
00:01:16,215 --> 00:01:17,422
ALICE: and they laugh.

28
00:01:17,442 --> 00:01:19,097
ALICE: And I just float above them.

29
00:01:19,117 --> 00:01:22,622
ALICE: Watching. I'm there... but I'm not really there.

30
00:01:23,514 --> 00:01:27,925
SIMMER: You know what I've learned, after three hundred years of soup?

31
00:01:27,945 --> 00:01:29,050
ALICE: ...What?

32
00:01:29,127 --> 00:01:33,070
SIMMER: Nobody remembers the soup. They remember who was sitting

33
00:01:33,090 --> 00:01:34,854
SIMMER: next to them.

34
00:01:34,874 --> 00:01:36,571
ALICE: But nobody can sit next to me.

35
00:01:36,591 --> 00:01:38,071
ALICE: I'm see-through.

36
00:01:38,294 --> 00:01:41,255
SIMMER: Then... we'll cook you a soup that's see-through, too.

37
00:01:41,275 --> 00:01:43,048
ALICE: A see-through... soup?

38
00:01:43,068 --> 00:01:44,623
SIMMER: Fetch me those sweets, dearie.

39
00:01:44,643 --> 00:01:46,023
SIMMER: The eyeball. The stripey one.

40
00:01:46,043 --> 00:01:48,523
SIMMER: Go on. Trust an old pot.

41
00:01:52,513 --> 00:01:55,775
The pot gave a gurgle, a burble, a sigh, and a silvery

42
00:01:55,795 --> 00:01:57,659
steam began curling up high.

43
00:01:57,679 --> 00:02:00,844
It was soft, it was swirly, it floated with grace...

44
00:02:00,864 --> 00:02:03,905
a wisp of a thing, with a rather sweet face.

45
00:02:03,925 --> 00:02:07,080
SIMMER: Steam is just soup that's learned how to float.

46
00:02:07,100 --> 00:02:10,330
SIMMER: Now... who does that remind you of?

47
00:02:10,476 --> 00:02:11,481
ALICE: ...Me?

48
00:02:11,669 --> 00:02:15,049
SIMMER: Go on, little one. Breathe it in.

49
00:02:19,008 --> 00:02:21,693
ALICE: Oh... Oh! It's staying... I can taste it!

50
00:02:21,713 --> 00:02:25,395
ALICE: It's minty... and sweet... and just a tiny bit eyeball-y...

51
00:02:25,415 --> 00:02:27,832
SIMMER: Ha! That's the secret ingredient!

52
00:02:27,852 --> 00:02:28,932
ALICE: Yum!

53
00:02:29,779 --> 00:02:32,526
And that Moon Supper night, at the end of the table, sat a

54
00:02:32,546 --> 00:02:35,923
ghost and a pot, just as close as they're able.

55
00:02:35,943 --> 00:02:39,023
ALICE: Granny Simmer? ...I don't feel hollow anymore.

56
00:02:39,149 --> 00:02:42,816
SIMMER: You never were, dearie. You were only waiting for someone

57
00:02:42,836 --> 00:02:44,624
SIMMER: to share with.

58
00:02:44,644 --> 00:02:47,674
ALICE: Then... save me a seat? Every Moon Supper?

59
00:02:47,713 --> 00:02:49,043
SIMMER: Always.

60
00:02:49,670 --> 00:02:52,150
So if ever you're feeling see-through, alone...

61
00:02:52,170 --> 00:02:54,861
like there's food on the table, but none is your own...

62
00:02:54,881 --> 00:02:57,745
remember small Alice, and Simmer's warm hum: it's who

63
00:02:57,765 --> 00:03:00,468
shares it with you... that makes it a yum.

64
00:03:00,488 --> 00:03:01,568
ALICE: Yum!

